# Java 11 Programming Standards

## Context

This rule defines standards and best practices for Java 11 development, ensuring consistent, maintainable, and efficient code.

## Standards

### 1. Language Features
- Use Java 11 features appropriately:
  - Local Variable Type Inference (var)
  - HTTP Client API
  - String methods (isBlank, strip, lines)
  - Collection factory methods
  - Optional improvements
  - Files methods (readString, writeString)

### 2. Code Structure
- Follow clean code principles
- Use meaningful variable names
- Keep methods small and focused
- Follow SOLID principles
- Use proper package structure

### 3. Performance
- Use appropriate data structures
- Implement efficient algorithms
- Handle resources properly
- Use parallel streams when beneficial
- Optimize memory usage

## Implementation Guidelines

### 1. Variable Declaration
```java
// Before
String message = "Hello";
List<String> names = new ArrayList<>();

// After (Java 11)
var message = "Hello";
var names = new ArrayList<String>();
```

### 2. String Operations
```java
// Before
if (str.trim().isEmpty()) {
    // handle empty
}

// After (Java 11)
if (str.isBlank()) {
    // handle empty
}

// Before
String[] lines = text.split("\n");

// After (Java 11)
List<String> lines = text.lines().collect(Collectors.toList());
```

### 3. HTTP Client
```java
// Before (Java 8)
HttpURLConnection connection = (HttpURLConnection) url.openConnection();
connection.setRequestMethod("GET");
try (BufferedReader reader = new BufferedReader(
        new InputStreamReader(connection.getInputStream()))) {
    String response = reader.lines().collect(Collectors.joining());
}

// After (Java 11)
HttpClient client = HttpClient.newHttpClient();
HttpRequest request = HttpRequest.newBuilder()
    .uri(URI.create("https://api.example.com"))
    .GET()
    .build();
HttpResponse<String> response = client.send(request, 
    HttpResponse.BodyHandlers.ofString());
```

### 4. Collection Factory Methods
```java
// Before
List<String> list = new ArrayList<>();
list.add("one");
list.add("two");
list.add("three");

// After (Java 11)
List<String> list = List.of("one", "two", "three");
Set<String> set = Set.of("one", "two", "three");
Map<String, Integer> map = Map.of("one", 1, "two", 2);
```

### 5. File Operations
```java
// Before
String content = new String(Files.readAllBytes(path));

// After (Java 11)
String content = Files.readString(path);
Files.writeString(path, content);
```

## Best Practices

### 1. Type Inference
- Use `var` for local variables when type is obvious
- Avoid `var` when type is not clear from context
- Don't use `var` for method parameters or return types

### 2. HTTP Client
- Use HttpClient for all HTTP operations
- Implement proper error handling
- Use appropriate timeouts
- Handle responses efficiently

### 3. String Operations
- Use new String methods for better readability
- Prefer `isBlank()` over `trim().isEmpty()`
- Use `lines()` for line-based operations
- Use `strip()` for Unicode-aware trimming

### 4. Collections
- Use factory methods for immutable collections
- Prefer List.of() over Arrays.asList()
- Use Set.of() for unique elements
- Use Map.of() for small maps

### 5. File Operations
- Use new Files methods for simple operations
- Handle exceptions properly
- Use appropriate character encoding
- Close resources properly

## Examples

### 1. Complete Class Example
```java
public class UserService {
    private final HttpClient httpClient;
    
    public UserService() {
        this.httpClient = HttpClient.newBuilder()
            .connectTimeout(Duration.ofSeconds(10))
            .build();
    }
    
    public User getUser(String id) {
        var request = HttpRequest.newBuilder()
            .uri(URI.create("https://api.example.com/users/" + id))
            .GET()
            .build();
            
        try {
            var response = httpClient.send(request, 
                HttpResponse.BodyHandlers.ofString());
            return parseUser(response.body());
        } catch (Exception e) {
            throw new UserServiceException("Failed to get user", e);
        }
    }
    
